ller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"MIN","distance":0.7238,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Kevin O'Connell"},{"season":2022,"team":"LV","distance":0.7684,"shared":21,"play_caller":"Josh McDaniels"},{"season":2025,"team":"SF","distance":0.7781,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Kyle Shanahan"}],"defense":[{"season":2023,"team":"GB","distance":0.6246,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"PIT","distance":0.6267,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2019,"team":"NYG","distance":0.6324,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"SEA","distance":0.6578,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"NYG","distance":0.6837,"shared":22,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Patriots went 14-3 in 2025 and outscored opponents 490 to 320. The offense carried the team, ranking 1st in EPA per play while the defense finished 11th in EPA allowed. What set them apart was the lowest screen rate and the 2nd lowest Cover 6 rate in the league.","offense":"The Patriots had one of the most efficient offenses in the league, ranking in the top eight in four of the five main efficiency measures. They threw more than almost anyone relative to the situation, even when the score did not call for it. Schematically, they stood out for the lowest screen rate and the 3rd lowest RPO rate in the league. Drake Maye ran the passing game, with Stefon Diggs leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Patriots defense was a little above average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was explosive play rate allowed, where it ranked 10th, and its weakest was pressure rate, where it ranked 25th. Harold Landry led the team with 8.5 sacks, and K'Lavon Chaisson added 7.5, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Schematically, they had the 2nd lowest Cover 6 rate in the league.","special":"The Patriots were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 84% of field goals, which was a little below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into running backs, about 8% of the money on the books.","players":"Drake Maye played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 4,394 yards. Targets were spread around, with Stefon Diggs and Hunter Henry leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. The backfield was a committee led by TreVeyon Henderson and Rhamondre Stevenson. On defense, Harold Landry led the team with 8.5 sacks and Jaylinn Hawkins picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"Josh McDaniels called the offense, and the defensive play caller for this season is not in the coaching data. Their offense looked most like the 2023 Texans under Bobby Slowik and the 2023 Raiders. Their defense looked most like the 2023 Packers and the 2024 Steelers.","trends":"The offense went from 27th in EPA per play in 2024 to 1st in 2025, a big step forward. The defense moved from 29th in EPA allowed per play in 2024 to 11th in 2025, a big step forward. The biggest change in approach was RPO rate, which went from 7% to 1%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2025. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}